      (a token callback) can never see half-updated books (read-only reentrancy, §15 rule 12). Off-chain reads and
      ordinary calls are never affected.
 @custom:security-contact See SECURITY.md

@notice Moves `value` from `from` to the caller, with `from`'s signed authorisation.
 @param from The authorising holder.
 @param to The payee, which must be the caller.
 @param value The amount, in base units.
 @param validAfter The authorisation is valid only after this time.
 @param validBefore The authorisation is valid only before this time.
 @param nonce A unique id: the contract uses the position id, so each authorisation funds one position, once.
 @param signature `from`'s signature of the authorisation (EIP-712), or a contract wallet's.

@title IUSDG
 @author Manic
 @notice The one USDG function the contract calls beyond ERC-20: EIP-3009's `receiveWithAuthorization`, in the form
         that takes the signature as bytes, so a contract wallet's (ERC-1271) authorisation works too.
 @dev Paxos's USDG on Robinhood Chain is built from facets. This form's selector, `0x88b7ab63`, is registered to its
      EIP-3009 facet (checked live on mainnet 4663, 2026-11-11). Only the payee, `to`, may submit it, so nobody can
      front-run the contract's pull. A nonce that was already used or cancelled is a silent no-op: no revert and no
      transfer. That is why `open` checks its own balance afterwards (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §8.2).
 @custom:security-contact See SECURITY.md

@notice Counts one call's winnings and losses in the current window, and trips the breaker when the window's
         net profit goes over its limit.
 @dev A count of nothing changes nothing. Otherwise:
      - fixed windows: once `windowStart + windowSecs` has come, both counters restart at zero, and the window
        starts at `currentTime`. Up to twice the limit can pass across a window's end, which §8.11 accepts;
      - both counters grow by the amounts (`MathOverflow` if one doesn't fit 64 bits);
      - a breaker with a limit that hasn't tripped trips when `profitOut > lossIn + maxNetProfit`, summed in 256
        bits, so even the largest limit can't overflow the check.
      The counters run while the breaker is off (a limit of 0) or tripped, so they always show the window's
      flows.
 @param b The breaker.
 @param profit The profit the call paid to users, before fees.
 @param loss The users' losses the call kept in the pool, before fees.
 @param currentTime The chain's clock.
 @return tripped True if this count tripped the breaker: it hadn't tripped before, and has now.

@notice Clears a trip and starts a fresh window at `currentTime`, with both counters at zero.
 @dev The limit and the window's length stay.
 @param b The breaker.
 @param currentTime The chain's clock.

@notice `x + y`, reverting `MathOverflow` when the sum doesn't fit 64 bits.
 @param x The counter.
 @param y The amount to add.
 @return The sum.

@title BreakerLib
 @author Manic
 @notice The payout circuit breaker's window and trip logic (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §8.11), as internal functions on
         the stored `Breaker`. It adds up, in fixed windows, the profit users take out of the pool and the losses
         they leave in it, both before fees, and trips when the profit out is more than the losses in plus the
         limit. A trip stops `open` and `sell` until the operator resets it (ADR 0003).
 @dev `FlashOptionBase` counts each call through `count` and emits the trip; `AdminModule` resets through `reset`.
      Both write only the breaker's first slot (§6.5b), so counting a call dirties one slot; the limit, alone in
      the second slot, is only read, and only while the breaker hasn't tripped. Kept out of the modules
      (ADR 0006), so its rules can be tested alone, without a proxy.
 @custom:security-contact See SECURITY.md

@notice The widest clock skew `validate` accepts, in seconds (ADR 0009): 6 times the launch value (5) and half
         the shortest bet (60). A skew lets a tick be that far ahead of the chain's clock, so a bet can be
         settled that early; a lower one only delays opens and settles while the chain's clock lags.

@notice Reverts unless a new bet's terms fit its market's settings.
 @dev The checks, in this order:
      - minStake ≤ stake ≤ maxStake (`InvalidAmount`);
      - the duration is an enabled rule's (`InvalidLeverageParams`). `validate` keeps an unused rule all zero, so
        it is never enabled and never matches, not even a zero duration;
      - that rule's thetaMinE7 ≤ thetaE7 ≤ thetaMaxE7 (`InvalidLeverageParams`);
      - floorMinBps ≤ floorBps ≤ floorMaxBps (`InvalidLeverageParams`);
      - max(capMinBps, floorBps) ≤ capBps ≤ capMaxBps (`InvalidLeverageParams`): a right call must be able to
        pay at least the floor.
 @param m The market's settings, as stored.
 @param stake The margin, in base units.
 @param durationSecs The bet's length.
 @param thetaE7 The liquidation distance θ × 1e7.
 @param floorBps The least a right call pays, in bps of the stake.
 @param capBps The most the bet pays, in bps of the stake.

@notice Reverts `InvalidLeverageParams` unless every bound in `p` is consistent at the market's leverage.
 @dev The checks, in this order:
      - the leverage is 1x to 10,000x (`LeverageMath.leverageAllowed`);
      - 0 < minStake ≤ maxStake;
      - 10_000 < floorMinBps ≤ floorMaxBps: a floor must pay back more than the stake;
      - 10_000 < capMinBps ≤ capMaxBps ≤ 40_000, and floorMaxBps ≤ capMaxBps: every floor fits under a cap;
        and a short can reach the highest cap (`LeverageMath.capReachable`), which only binds below 4x;
      - maxSellFeeBps ≤ 10_000: an early-sell fee can never take more than the valuation;
      - each duration rule is either all zero (an unused slot), or has 0 < thetaMinE7 ≤ thetaMaxE7, a widest
        line below 1/leverage (`LeverageMath.thetaWithinLimit`: under 10 bp at 1000x, 20 bp at 500x, 100 bp
        at 100x), and a duration no earlier rule has. A disabled rule is still checked, so it can be switched
        on later safely.
      Then three more bounds, last (`withinEvmBounds`):
      - entryMaxAgeSecs > 0: a market accepting no price age could never open a bet;
      - 0 < maxExposureBps ≤ 10_000: the open gate is on, at 100% of free money at most. Then free money
        always covers the open bets' max profit, so an IOU can't form (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §12, invariant 5).
        Only an upgrade could switch the gate off (0) or set it above 100%;
      - clockSkewSecs ≤ `MAX_CLOCK_SKEW_SECS`, so no bet can be settled more than 30 seconds early.
      The last two keep a leaked operator key, which acts at once, from loosening what pays out (ADR 0009).
      The sell window, the owed limit (`maxTotalOwed`) and `enabled` are left unbounded. Every refusal is the
      same error, so the order only matters for gas.
 @param p The settings to check.
 @param leverage The market's leverage L.

@notice The market's enabled duration rule for `durationSecs`.
 @dev Reverts `InvalidLeverageParams` when there is none. `validate` allows no two rules with the same duration,
      so the first match is the only one.
 @param m The market's settings.
 @param durationSecs The bet's length.
 @return The matching rule.

@notice Whether duration rule `i` is valid: all zero if unused, otherwise a θ range above 0 and below
         1/leverage, and a duration no earlier rule has.
 @param rules The market's five duration rules.
 @param i The rule to check.
 @param leverage The market's leverage, which sets the widest line.
 @return Whether the rule is valid.

@notice Whether `p` passes the three bounds `validate` checks last: a price age above 0, the open gate on at
         100% at most, and a clock skew of at most `MAX_CLOCK_SKEW_SECS` (ADR 0009).
 @param p The settings to check.
 @return Whether all three hold.

@title ConfigLib
 @author Manic
 @notice The checks on a market's settings, and on each new bet's terms against them. `validate` checks the
         settings at the market's leverage.
 @dev `validate` runs in `createMarket` and `setConfig` before anything is stored. `validateOpen` checks each new
      bet's terms against the stored settings.
 @custom:security-contact See SECURITY.md

@notice Divides `a` by `b`, rounding toward minus infinity.
 @dev Solidity's signed `/` rounds toward zero: `-7 / 2 == -3`, where the floor is -4. For a losing price move
      that difference is money: rounding toward zero would round the loss in the user's favour. The callers
      always divide by a positive price, so `type(int256).min / -1` (which reverts) can't occur.
 @param a The dividend.
 @param b The divisor; must not be 0 (division by zero panics).
 @return q The largest integer `q` with `q × b <= a` when `b > 0`.

@notice Whether `leverage` is in the allowed range, `MIN_LEVERAGE` to `MAX_LEVERAGE` (1x to 10,000x).
 @param leverage The leverage L, a whole number (1000 is 1000x).
 @return True if the maths accepts it.

@notice Whether a liquidation distance θ stays below 1/leverage, so the move to a position's line costs less
         than its whole stake: `thetaE7 × leverage < 1e7`. That is under 10 bp at 1000x, 20 bp at 500x and
         100 bp at 100x.
 @dev At the line the price has moved θ against the user, so the payout multiple is 1 − L × θ, which stays
      above 0 only while L × θ < 1. The product is taken in 256 bits, where no `uint32` θ times a `uint16`
      leverage can overflow.
 @param thetaE7 The liquidation distance θ × 1e7.
 @param leverage The leverage L.
 @return True if the line comes before a move that would take the whole stake.

@notice Whether a short can reach the cap: `capBps − 10_000 < leverage × 10_000`, that is cap − 1x < L.
 @dev A short's payout grows by L × the fall in price, and a price can't fall below 0, so a short gains at most
      L × its stake: a higher cap would put its cap line at or below 0. From 4x leverage up every cap up to the
      4x ceiling passes, so the rule only binds at 1x, 2x and 3x. A cap below 1x counts as 1x (a gain of 0);
      `lines` and the config check reject such a cap anyway.
 @param capBps The payout cap, in bps of the stake.
 @param leverage The leverage L.
 @return True if a short's cap line sits above 0.

@notice The liquidation and cap prices of a position opened at `entry` with `leverage`.
 @dev The checks run in this order: the price, then the parameters.
      - The liquidation line sits θ away from entry, whatever the leverage: `entry × thetaE7 / 1e7`. At
        $83,884.00, θ = 2.627 bp puts it $22.04 away.
      - The cap line is where the payout multiple reaches the cap: 1 + L × move = cap, so the move is
        (cap − 1x) / L, which is `entry × (capBps − 10_000) / (L × 10_000)`. At 1000x a 4x cap is a
        0.3% move ($251.65 from the same entry), at 500x a 2.5x cap is the same 0.3%, and at 100x a 2x cap is
        1%.
      Both offsets have positive operands, so truncating is flooring: each line sits at most one price unit
      closer to entry. A long loses when the price falls, so its line is below entry and its cap above; a short
      mirrors it.
 @param entry The entry price; must be positive (`InvalidPrice`).
 @param side `Call` (long) or `Put` (short).
 @param leverage The leverage L: `leverageAllowed` (`InvalidLeverageParams`).
 @param thetaE7 The liquidation distance θ × 1e7: positive, and `thetaWithinLimit` at L
        (`InvalidLeverageParams`).
 @param capBps The payout cap: `10_000 < capBps <= MAX_LEVERAGE_CAP_BPS`, and `capReachable` at L
        (`InvalidLeverageParams`).
 @return liqPrice Touching this price (equality counts) liquidates the position.
 @return capPrice The cap line, rounded toward the entry: the payout stops growing at this price, or one price
         unit beyond it when the division isn't exact. `MathOverflow` if it doesn't fit `int64`.

@notice The cap line of a position opened at `entry` with `leverage`: the price, rounded toward the entry,
         where its payout stops growing (one price unit beyond it when the division isn't exact). The second line
 of `lines` on its own, for a position whose θ isn't stored (`getPosition`).
 @dev The same checks and the same arithmetic as `lines`' cap line, which doesn't use θ: the price, then the
      leverage and the cap, then `entry ± entry × (capBps − 10_000) / (L × 10_000)`, rounded toward entry.
      A fuzz test proves it equals `lines`' cap price for every entry, side, leverage and cap. Named for the
      line, since `capPrice` is `lines`' return.
 @param entry The entry price; must be positive (`InvalidPrice`).
 @param side `Call` (the cap price is above entry) or `Put` (below).
 @param leverage The leverage L: `leverageAllowed` (`InvalidLeverageParams`).
 @param capBps The payout cap: `10_000 < capBps <= MAX_LEVERAGE_CAP_BPS`, and `capReachable` at L
        (`InvalidLeverageParams`).
 @return The cap price. `MathOverflow` if it doesn't fit `int64`.

@notice The payout multiple g at `price`, in bps of the stake, for a position at `leverage` whose line was not
         touched.
 @dev `R` is the move in the user's favour relative to entry, × L × 10_000, floored: L × the move in bps. At
      1000x, +1 bp gives +1_000, so a +1 bp move pays 1.1x (11_000) and a -1 bp move 0.9x (9_000); at 100x, +1 bp
      gives +100: 1.01x. g = 10_000 + R. Any winning move, however small (`R > 0`), pays at least the floor. g
      never exceeds the cap and never goes below 0; only a move past the liquidation line could go that low, and
      such a position is liquidated instead of settled. The prices are checked before the leverage.
 @param entry The entry price; must be positive (`InvalidPrice`).
 @param price The price to value the position at; must be positive (`InvalidPrice`).
 @param side `Call` (long) or `Put` (short).
 @param leverage The leverage L: `leverageAllowed` (`InvalidLeverageParams`).
 @param floorBps The least a winning position pays, in bps of its stake (e.g. 13_000 = 1.3x).
 @param capBps The most it pays, in bps of its stake (e.g. 40_000 = 4x).
 @return The payout multiple in bps, between 0 and `capBps`.

@notice What a position pays at multiple `multipleBps`: `stake × multipleBps / 10_000`.
 @param stake The position's stake, in token base units.
 @param multipleBps The payout multiple, in bps (from `gBps`).
 @return The payout, rounded down (pool favoured). `MathOverflow` if it doesn't fit `uint64`.

@notice The most a position can win above its stake: `stake × (capBps − 10_000) / 10_000`. Opening a position
         adds it to the pool's open exposure, and ending it subtracts the same value.
 @param stake The position's stake, in token base units.
 @param capBps The position's payout cap; must be at least 10_000 (`InvalidLeverageParams`). Note the bound is
        `>=` here but `>` in `lines`: a 1x cap has a max profit of 0.
 @return The max profit, rounded down. `MathOverflow` if it doesn't fit `uint64`.

@notice Whether `price` touches the liquidation line. Equality counts.
 @param price The price to check.
 @param liqPrice The position's liquidation line.
 @param side `Call`: touched at or below the line. `Put`: touched at or above it.
 @return True if the position is liquidated at `price`.

@notice Checks that a price tick liquidates a position.
 @dev The checks run in this order: a positive price; the tick inside the position's life, both ends included;
      the tick no further ahead of the chain's clock than the skew; and the price touching the line (equality
      counts). Old ticks are accepted, so the backend can replay after an outage. The price and the tick time
      are the same for a whole batch, so their errors carry no id; the two that depend on the position do.
      The comparisons never add or subtract past zero, so they can't overflow.
 @param positionId The position being liquidated, named in its errors.
 @param startTime The position's entry tick time.
 @param endTime The position's expiry time.
 @param liqPrice The position's liquidation line.
 @param side `Call` (liquidated at or below the line) or `Put` (at or above).
 @param price The tick's price; must be positive (`InvalidPrice`).
 @param publishTime The tick's time: `startTime <= publishTime <= endTime` (`InvalidLiquidationWindow`).
 @param currentTime The chain's clock (`block.timestamp`).
 @param clockSkewSecs How far ahead of the clock a tick may be (`PriceFromFuture`).

@notice Checks the time of the price tick an open or a sale uses.
 @dev The checks run in this order: a tick time at all (0 is `PriceTooOld`); no further ahead of the clock than
      the skew; no older than the maximum age. A tick a little ahead of the clock counts as age 0.
 @param currentTime The chain's clock (`block.timestamp`), used only as a sanity bound.
 @param publishTime The tick's time.
 @param maxAgeSecs The oldest a tick may be, in seconds (`PriceTooOld`).
 @param clockSkewSecs How far ahead of the clock a tick may be (`PriceFromFuture`).

@notice The open gate.
 @dev A new position is rejected while the total owed exceeds `maxTotalOwed`, and, when `maxExposureBps > 0`,
      when the open positions' max profit plus this one's would exceed `free × maxExposureBps / 10_000`. The
      limit rounds down, so the gate errs on the pool's side. At launch `maxExposureBps` is 10_000: the open
      max profit can never exceed free money, so every winner can be paid in full. 0 turns the gate off here,
      but `ConfigLib.validate` keeps a market's value at 1 to 10_000 (ADR 0009).
 @param totalOwed The pool's total owed to winners.
 @param maxTotalOwed The market's limit on it (`OpenBlockedTotalOwed`).
 @param openMaxProfit The sum of the open positions' max profit.
 @param newMaxProfit The new position's max profit.
 @param free The pool's free money (`total - lockedMargin`).
 @param maxExposureBps The exposure limit in bps of free money; 0 turns it off (`OpenBlockedExposure`).

@notice `amount × feeBps / 10_000`.
 @dev Rounds down, so a fee never rounds against the user, and the rate is clamped to 100%, so a fee never
      exceeds `amount`.
 @param amount The amount the fee is taken from.
 @param feeBps The fee rate in bps (values above 10_000 count as 10_000).
 @return The fee.

@notice The 2% winner fee on profit actually paid to the user.
 @dev Taken at settle, on a profitable sale and on each repayment. Profit that is only owed pays no fee until it
      is repaid.
 @param profitPaid The profit paid out now.
 @return The winner fee.

@notice The 3% loser fee on `loss`, taken only while the pool's free money is at or above its floor.
 @dev `freeBefore` is the free money before this order. A queue only forms once free money has run out, and
      repayments spend new losers' money on it, so the fee is usually skipped while winners are owed.
 @param loss The loss (the whole stake when liquidated).
 @param freeBefore The pool's free money before this order.
 @param poolFloor The pool's floor.
 @return The loser fee, or 0 below the floor.

@notice Splits an expiry payout into the user's own stake, profit paid now, profit owed, and the fees.
 @dev The user's own stake comes back first: all of it on a win, what is left of it on a loss. Anything above
      the stake is profit, which only free money can pay. While anyone is owed (`totalOwed > 0`), a new
      winner's whole profit joins the queue, so nobody jumps it.
 @param payoutAmount What the position pays at expiry (`payout(stake, gBps(...))`).
 @param stake The position's stake.
 @param freeBefore The pool's free money with this position's stake still held.
 @param totalOwed The pool's total owed before this settle.
 @param poolFloor The pool's floor, for the loser fee.
 @return split The split. See `SettleSplit`.

@notice What the user receives now from a settle: `own + paid - winnerFee`.
 @param split A settle split.
 @return The amount for the user.

@notice What leaves the pool's books on a settle: the user's part and both fees, `own + paid + loserFee`.
 @param split A settle split.
 @return The amount that leaves the pool's `total`.

@notice Checks that a sale's tick falls inside the position's sell window: at least `minDelaySecs` after the
         start, and more than `cutoffSecs` before the end.
 @dev Written as comparisons that can't overflow: a tick before the start is too early, and an end at or below
      the cutoff closes the window, so neither subtraction goes below zero.
 @param startTime The position's entry tick time.
 @param endTime The position's expiry time.
 @param publishTime The sale's tick time.
 @param minDelaySecs How long after the start a sale is first allowed.
 @param cutoffSecs How long before the end sales stop (`SellWindowClosed` for every bound).

@notice Checks the amounts of an early sale.
 @dev The checks run in this order: the valuation against what the position pays at this price; the sale
      amount against the valuation; the early-sell fee (`valuation - sellAmount`) against its cap; the user's
      minimum; and that the pool can pay the sale in full right now: nobody owed, and the valuation within free
      money plus the position's own stake. A sale is never partly paid.
 @param stake The position's stake.
 @param gNowBps The position's payout multiple at the sale price.
 @param valuation What the backend values the position at (`ValuationAboveCurrentPayout`).
 @param sellAmount What the user gets before the winner fee (`SellAmountExceedsValuation`, `SellFeeTooHigh`).
 @param minAmount The least the user signed for (`SellAmountBelowMin`).
 @param maxSellFeeBps The market's early-sell fee cap, in bps of the valuation.
 @param totalOwed The pool's total owed (`QueueNotEmpty`).
 @param freeBefore The pool's free money with this position's stake still held (`SellNotPayable`).

@notice Splits a sale that `checkSell` accepted.
 @dev The winner fee is 2% of the profit the user takes (`sellAmount - stake`, if positive). The loser fee is 3%
      of the loss in the valuation (`stake - valuation`, if positive), measured against the floor with
      `freeBefore`, as at settle. Requires `sellAmount <= valuation`, which `checkSell` guarantees.
 @param stake The position's stake.
 @param valuation The accepted valuation.
 @param sellAmount The accepted sale amount.
 @param freeBefore The pool's free money with this position's stake still held.
 @param poolFloor The pool's floor, for the loser fee.
 @return split The split. See `SellSplit`.

@notice What goes to the fee recipient on a sale: the early-sell fee and the winner or loser fee.
 @param split A sell split.
 @return The total fees.

@notice What leaves the pool's books on a sale: `toUser + fees`, which equals the valuation plus the loser fee.
 @param split A sell split.
 @return The amount that leaves the pool's `total`.

@notice Repays an IOU of `owed` from free money (never from open stakes), as much as free money allows, with
         the 2% winner fee on each repayment.
 @param owed What the IOU still owes.
 @param free The pool's free money.
 @return split The split. See `RepaySplit`.

@notice Checks an admin withdrawal from the pool.
 @dev The checks run in this order: a positive amount; nobody owed; free money staying at or above the floor,
      which also rejects an amount above free money; what is left above the floor still covering every open
      position's max profit, so a withdrawal never leaves open bets unpayable (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §8.1);
      and, for seed recovery, at most 500 whole tokens in total.
 @param purpose Seed recovery (capped) or rewards (not capped).
 @param amount The amount to withdraw (`InvalidAmount` if 0).
 @param free The pool's free money (`total - lockedMargin`), so open stakes can never be withdrawn.
 @param totalOwed The pool's total owed (`OwedOutstanding`).
 @param poolFloor The pool's floor (`BelowPoolFloor`).
 @param openMaxProfit The sum of the open positions' max profit (`OpenExposureNotCovered`).
 @param seedRecovered The seed recovered so far (`SeedRecoveryExceeded`).
 @param decimals The token's decimals, which scale the 500-token cap (`MathOverflow` if it can't fit).

@dev `tokens` whole tokens in base units (`tokens × 10^decimals`), reverting `MathOverflow` when `10^decimals`
      or the product doesn't fit `uint64`. `10^20` already exceeds `uint64`, so larger decimals stop early,
      before `10**decimals` could overflow even 256 bits.

@title LeverageMath
 @author Manic
 @notice The Flash Option's maths: a position's liquidation and cap lines, its payout multiple, the fees, the
         money splits of a settle, a sale and a repayment, and the checks on price times, the open gate, sales and
         withdrawals. Every function is pure, and the backend computes the same numbers off-chain. docs/MATH.md
         states each rule, with worked examples.
 @dev Units:
      - prices: `int64` at the market's price exponent (BTC: -8, so $83,884 is 8_388_400_000_000);
      - amounts: `uint64` base units of the token (USDG has 6 decimals, so $10 is 10_000_000);
      - the leverage L: a whole number from 1 to 10,000 (1000 is 1000x);
      - payout multiples (g, floor, cap) and fee rates: basis points, where 10_000 is 1x or 100%;
      - the liquidation distance θ: `thetaE7 = θ × 1e7`, so 2.627 bp is 2_627.
      The maths runs in 256-bit integers, where none of these values can overflow, and each result is narrowed
      once at the end with a range check that reverts `MathOverflow`. Every division rounds down (toward minus
      infinity in `gBps`, whose dividend is negative on a losing move), which is always in the pool's favour.
      The functions come in this order: `floorDiv`; the leverage checks (`leverageAllowed`, `thetaWithinLimit`,
      `capReachable`); the lines and the payout (`lines`, `capLine`, `gBps`, `payout`, `maxProfit`, `touched`);
      the time checks; the open gate; the fees; the settle split; the sale; the repay split; and the withdrawal
      check.
 @custom:security-contact See SECURITY.md

@notice The EIP-712 struct hash of an order to open a position.
 @param o The order.
 @return The struct hash.

@notice The EIP-712 struct hash of an order to sell a position early.
 @param s The order.
 @return The struct hash.

@title OrderLib
 @author Manic
 @notice The EIP-712 type hashes and struct hashes of the two orders a user signs: `OpenOrder` and `SellOrder`
         (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §7).
 @dev A struct hash is EIP-712's `hashStruct`: keccak256 of the type hash followed by each field, in the type
      string's order, each encoded as 32 bytes (`side` as a `uint8`). The contract turns it into the digest the user
      signed with `_hashTypedDataV4`, which adds the domain: the name, the version, the chain id and the proxy's
      address. The backend hashes the same structs, so test/fixtures/eip712_vectors.json pins every type hash and
      struct hash, computed by an independent EIP-712 implementation.
 @custom:security-contact See SECURITY.md

@notice Writes every setting of `p` into a market.
 @dev The market's id, pause and price exponent are not settings and are left alone.
 @param m The market's storage.
 @param p The settings, already validated.

@notice Engaging a brake (a pause or the halt) needs the guardian or the operator.
 @dev Reverts OpenZeppelin's `AccessControlUnauthorizedAccount`, naming `GUARDIAN_ROLE`: the role made for
      braking. The operator may brake too, because the role that releases a brake can always re-engage it.

@notice Checks a market's settings with `ConfigLib.validate`, at the market's leverage.
 @dev A private function, so the library's checks are compiled once for both `createMarket` and `setConfig`.
      An internal library function is inlined at every call site, and this one is about 1 KB.
 @param p The settings to check.
 @param leverage The market's leverage, which sets the limits on its lines and caps.

@title AdminModule
 @author Manic
 @notice The admin functions: creating markets and changing their settings, the two brakes (pause a market, halt
         everything), the pool floor, the recipients, the safety refund's delay, the payout circuit breaker's
         controls, and the guardian's revoke of a relayer key (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §8.1, §8.9b, §8.11).
 @dev Who may do what (§4.2):
      - the admin (the 48-hour timelock on mainnet) creates markets, and sets the floor, the recipients, the
        refund delay and the circuit breaker's limit and window;
      - the operator (the admin Safe, instant) changes market settings, releases the brakes and resets the
        circuit breaker;
      - the guardian or the operator engages the brakes;
      - the guardian revokes relayer keys.
      None of these functions makes an external call. Each is `nonReentrant` all the same, like every
      state-changing function, and the guard costs little with transient storage.
 @custom:security-contact See SECURITY.md

@title PoolModule
 @author Manic
 @notice The pool's money in and out: funding, the admin's withdrawals, claims of payouts that waited, and fee sweeps
         (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §8.1, §8.8).
 @dev These are the only places tokens enter or leave outside a bet. `withdraw`, `claim` and `sweepFees` follow
      checks, then effects, then the transfer, with the event before the transfer; a failed transfer reverts all of
      it, so nothing is lost. `fund` pulls first, because it must measure what actually arrived. Every function is
      `nonReentrant`, and none checks the halt or a pause (§8.10).
 @custom:security-contact See SECURITY.md

@notice Liquidates one open position at the batch's tick, and counts it and its fee in the batch.
 @dev The loser fee is measured against free money with the stake still held, so it is computed before the
      margin is released. Emits {PositionLiquidated} after the position closes, from the stake and the line
      read before slot 1 was cleared.
 @param b The batch.
 @param id The position's id.
 @param p The position's storage.

@notice Settles one open position at the batch's tick, pays its user, and counts it and its fees in the
         batch.
 @dev The event's fields are gathered in one memory struct, so the function fits the legacy pipeline's stack.
      Emits {PositionSettled} once the books are done and before the payout, whose transfer is the only external
      call (checks, effects, then the interaction).
 @param b The batch.
 @param id The position's id.
 @param p The position's storage.

@notice Returns one open position's whole stake to its user, once the refund delay has passed since its
         expiry: the safety refund (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §8.6).
 @dev The expiry plus the delay is summed in 256 bits, so no delay can overflow it: one too long to reach just
      means "not yet". The stake and the max profit are released and the stake leaves the total, so free money
      doesn't change; no fee is taken. Emits {PositionRefunded} once the books are written and before the
      payout, the only external call, as settle does.
 @param id The position's id.
 @param p The position's storage.
 @param delay The refund delay, in seconds; not 0.

@notice Repays the head of the IOU queue as much as free money allows.
 @dev Nothing is written when there is no free money: the caller stops. Paid in full, the IOU leaves the queue
      (its entry deleted, the head moved on) and the position closes with slots 1 and 2 cleared; paid in part,
      it keeps the rest owed at the head. Emits {OwedRepaid} once the books are written and before the payout,
      the only external call.
 @param pool The pool's books.
 @return split What the head was repaid: nothing (`amount` 0) when there is no free money.

@notice Turns a settled position whose profit the pool can't pay now into an IOU at the back of the repay
         queue.
 @dev The position keeps slot 1 and gets slot 2: what it is owed and its place in the queue. Each counter is
      checked (`MathOverflow`).
 @param pool The pool's books.
 @param p The position's storage.
 @param id The position's id, which joins the queue.
 @param owed The profit owed.
 @return queueSeq The IOU's place in the queue.

@notice The checks every batch of `liquidate` and `settle` runs first: the halt, the batch's size and the
         market (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §8.4, §8.5). Then the batch, with nothing ended yet.
 @param marketId The batch's market's feed.
 @param leverage The batch's market's leverage: a batch holds one market, so one leverage.
 @param price The tick's price.
 @param publishTime The tick's publish time.
 @param count The number of ids in the batch.
 @return b The batch.

@notice Looks up one id of a batch: it must have been opened, on the batch's market.
 @dev Reverts `UnknownPosition(id)` for an id never opened, and `ConfigMismatch(id)` for a position of another
      market, so a bad id names itself and the backend can resend without it.
 @param id The position's id.
 @param index The batch's market index.
 @return p The position's storage.

@notice Looks up a position that must have been opened, on any market.
 @dev Reverts `UnknownPosition(id)` for an id never opened. Every opened id keeps its record, ended or not.
 @param id The position's id.
 @return p The position's storage.

@notice Writes the new position and holds its stake and max profit in the pool's books.
 @dev The position takes slots 0 and 1. Slot 2 is written only if the position later becomes an IOU. The stake
      counts in the total and in the held margin, so free money is unchanged and no one else's payout can use it.
      Each counter is checked (`MathOverflow`).
 @param o The order.
 @param t The relayer's terms.
 @param index The market's index.
 @param leverage The market's leverage, which the bet keeps.
 @param liqPrice The liquidation line.
 @param maxProfit The position's max profit.

@notice Closes a sold position and moves the sale through the pool's books.
 @dev The stake and its max profit are released; the valuation and the loser fee leave the total (the user's
      part and every fee: `outOfPool`); `transferred` counts what the user gets; the early-sell, winner and loser
      fees accrue in the pending fees. Then the position closes (slot 0 kept, slot 1 cleared). Each counter is
      checked (`MathOverflow`).
 @param p The position's storage.
 @param split The sale's split.

@notice Pulls exactly the stake from the user, with their EIP-3009 authorisation.
 @dev Only the contract, the payee, can submit the authorisation, so nobody can front-run it, and nothing stays
      approved. The balance must rise by exactly the stake (`TransferAmountMismatch`). That also catches USDG's
      silent no-op when the nonce, the position id, was already used or cancelled: no revert and no transfer.
 @param o The order.
 @param a The user's authorisation.

@notice The checks on the order itself: the brakes, the deadline, the market, the position id and the
         user's signature (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §8.2, checks 1 to 5).
 @param o The order.
 @param signature The user's signature of it.
 @return m The market's storage.
 @return index The market's index, which the position records.

@notice The checks on the terms, in this order: the market's bounds, the entry tick's time and `notBefore`, the
         lines and the open gate (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §8.2, checks 6 to 10).
 @dev The position's clock starts at the entry tick, not when the transaction lands, so a slow transaction never
      lengthens a bet. Free money is the pool's total less the stakes it holds.
 @param m The market's storage.
 @param o The order.
 @param t The relayer's terms.
 @return e The fields of {PositionOpened}.
 @return maxProfit The most the position can win, which the pool must keep covered while it is open.

@notice The checks on the sale order and its tick: the brakes, the deadline, the position, the user's signature,
         the sell window, the tick's time and `notBefore` (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §8.3, checks 1 to 8).
 @param s The order.
 @param signature The position's user's signature of it.
 @param t The relayer's terms.
 @return p The position's storage.
 @return m Its market's storage.

@notice The checks on the price and the amounts, in this order, and the sale's split (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md
         §8.3, checks 9 and 10).
 @dev Free money is measured with the position's stake still held (`freeBefore`), for both the payability check
      and the loser fee. The relayer's valuation is bounded by what the position pays at this
      price (`gNow`), so a sale can never pay more than a settle at the same price would.
 @param p The position's storage.
 @param m Its market's storage.
 @param s The order.
 @param t The relayer's terms.
 @return e The fields of {PositionSold}.
 @return split The sale's split.

@title TradingModule
 @author Manic
 @notice Opening positions and selling them early: what the opener relayer calls with a user's signed order and the
         price tick it saw (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §8.2, §8.3).
 @dev `open` runs its checks in the order its interface lists, then writes the position and the books, emits
      {PositionOpened}, and only then pulls the stake. A failed pull reverts all of it. `sell` runs its checks the
      same way, then closes the position through the books helpers every ending shares, emits {PositionSold},
      counts the sale's profit or loss in the payout circuit breaker (`_countForBreaker`, §8.11), and only then
      pays the user through `_pay`, which never reverts on a refused transfer. The
      checks are split into private functions only to keep each within the legacy pipeline's stack. The private
      functions are listed in solhint's order, state-changing before view; `open` runs `_checkOrder`,
      `_checkTerms`, `_recordOpen`, `_emitOpened` and `_pullStake`, and `sell` runs `_checkSale`, `_priceSale`,
      `_recordSale` and `_emitSold`.
 @custom:security-contact See SECURITY.md

@title ViewsModule
 @author Manic
 @notice The contract's read-only functions (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §8.9): the getters and the EIP-712 hash helpers.
 @dev Every getter that reads the `Layout` carries OpenZeppelin's `nonReentrantView`, so it reverts while a
      state-changing function holds the reentrancy guard (§15 rule 12). The hash helpers read no books, so they need
      no guard. `version` stays in `ManicFlashOption`, the entry contract, beside everything else an upgrade changes.
 @custom:security-contact See SECURITY.md

@notice How an expiry payout splits, fees included.
 @dev The user receives `own + paid - winnerFee`, and `own + paid + loserFee` leaves the pool's books
      (`LeverageMath.toUser` and `LeverageMath.outOfPool`).

@notice How an early sale splits, fees included.
 @dev The fee recipient receives `sellFee + winnerFee + loserFee` (`LeverageMath.fees`), and that plus `toUser`
      leaves the pool's books (`LeverageMath.outOfPool`).

@notice An order to open a position, signed by the user (EIP-712) before the price is known.
 @dev EIP-712 type string, one line without spaces after the commas (`side` encodes as `uint8`):
      OpenOrder(bytes32 positionId,address user,bytes32 marketId,uint16 leverage,uint8 side,uint64 stake,
      uint16 durationSecs,uint48 notBefore,uint48 deadline)
      The user signs only the order, so the order must name the leverage, and with it the market (ADR 0008).

@notice The terms the relayer adds to an `OpenOrder` at the price tick.
 @dev `open` checks each one against the market's bounds and the order (§8.2), so a relayer can only pick terms
      inside what the admin allowed.

@notice The user's EIP-3009 `ReceiveWithAuthorization` for the stake: exactly `stake`, from the user to the
         contract, with the order's `positionId` as its nonce.
 @dev The token checks the signature (ERC-1271 wallets included). Only the receiving contract can submit it, so
      nobody can front-run it, and nothing is left approved afterwards.

@notice The terms the relayer adds to a `SellOrder` at the price tick.
 @dev `sell` checks them with `LeverageMath.checkSell` (§8.3): the valuation can't exceed the current payout,
      and the early-sell fee (`valuation - sellAmount`) can't exceed the market's maximum.

@notice A market's settings, as `createMarket` and `setConfig` take them (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §6.3, §8.1).
 @dev The same fields, in the same order, as `Market` without `marketId`, `priceExponent` and `leverage` (all
      fixed at creation) and `paused` (its own switch). `setConfig` replaces them all at once, and
      `ConfigLib.validate` checks them first.

@notice Everything `initialize` sets, in one struct (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §6.7, §8.1).
 @dev The role holders differ by network (§4.2). On mainnet the admin and the upgrader are the 48-hour
      `TimelockController`, and the operator is the admin Safe. On the testnet they may be the deployer.

@notice The direction of a bet: Call wins when the price rises ("Higher", a long), Put wins when it falls
         ("Lower", a short).
 @dev Stored as a `uint8` inside each position, so members can only ever be appended.

@notice What an admin withdrawal from the pool is for. Both need the pool above its floor and nobody owed.
 @dev `SeedRecovery`: taking back the launch seed, at most 500 whole tokens in total, to the fee recipient.
      `Rewards`: surplus paid back to users (for example competitions), to the rewards recipient. Carried as a
      `uint8` in the withdrawal event, so members can only ever be appended.

@notice Where a position is in its life: None → Open → (Owed →) Closed. A status only ever moves forward.
 @dev `None`: the id was never used. `Open`: running, its stake held in the pool. `Owed`: ended as a winner the
      pool couldn't pay in full, with an IOU in the repay queue. `Closed`: finished. A finished position's slot 0
      stays behind with `Closed` as a tombstone, so a `positionId`, which is also the order's nonce, can never be
      opened twice. Stored as a `uint8`, so members can only ever be appended.

@notice An allowed position duration and the range of liquidation distances θ a bet of that length may have.
         Longer bets get wider lines, since the price wanders further.
 @dev One slot (11 of 32 bytes). Embedded five times in `Market`, so it can never gain a field. The duration is a
      `uint16`: it allows up to about 18 hours, far more than the 1 to 5 minutes offered, and keeps `Position`
      slot 0 at exactly 32 bytes.

@notice One market's settings: the bounds every bet on it is checked against. One per price feed and leverage
         (ADR 0008), in `Layout.markets`: BTC can have a 1000x, a 500x and a 100x market, each with its own
         settings.
 @dev 8 slots. Slot 1 holds everything read on every open and sale. A market holds no token and no keys of its
      own: the token is global, and the roles act on every market. `ConfigParams` (types/ParamTypes.sol) is this
      struct without `marketId`, `paused`, `priceExponent` and `leverage`.

@notice One bet, in `Layout.positions` under its `positionId`.
 @dev 3 slots. Slot 0 is exactly 32 bytes and stays forever as the tombstone. Slot 1 is cleared when the position
      closes (a gas refund). Slot 2 is written only if the position becomes an IOU.
      Not stored:
      - the position id, which is the mapping key, and the feed, which `marketIdx` leads to;
      - the cap price and θ, which are never read after the open, so they are only in the `PositionOpened` event
        (the `getPosition` view recomputes the cap price);
      - the end time, which is `startTime + durationSecs`.

@notice The pool's books.
 @dev 4 slots, embedded in `Layout`, so it can never gain a field: new pool data goes at the end of `Layout`
      instead. Free money (`total - lockedMargin`) is computed each time, never stored. `feesPending` and
      `totalClaimable` are kept apart from `total`: fees accrue and are swept separately, and payouts that fail
      wait in `claimable`.

@notice The payout circuit breaker's counters and limit (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §8.11).
 @dev 2 slots, embedded in `Layout`, so it can never gain a field. It trips when the profit paid out in one window
      exceeds the losses kept by more than `maxNetProfit` (ADR 0003), and then blocks open and sell until the
      operator resets it. Ordered by access (§5 rule 7): everything a batch updates is in slot 0, so counting a
      batch writes one slot. `maxNetProfit` doesn't fit beside it, so it alone takes slot 1, which only
      `setBreaker` writes.
